{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779197969,"nonce":"Cnb9ys/Ryoh8iIeY3k0nt8BRpwI=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCuao6A","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/133","origin":"github"},"title":"OAK cameras: coprime keyframe intervals to decorrelate IDR peaks for Starlink-only ops","message":"## Problem\n\nPost-mission analysis of the 2026-05-01 deployment (#124,\n`~/data/analysis/2026-05-01_findings.md`) and 2026-05-18 pier link\ncalibration (`2026-05-18_starlink_only_budget.md`) show the dominant cause\nof Starlink-side camera drops is **IDR-keyframe coincidence across cameras**,\nnot average bitrate.\n\n- 3 OAK ffmpeg streams on VPN averaged **2.27 Mbps total** (≈ 800 kbps each)\n  — comfortably under the Starlink lossy knee (~5 Mbps @ 0.19% loss).\n- Per-camera **single-second peaks** hit oak_forward 14.8 / oak_port 7.9 /\n  oak_starboard 20.0 Mbps. H.265 IDR keyframes. At fps=5, each frame\n  carries ~160 kbits mean; a busy-scene IDR can run many × that, dominating\n  whatever 1-second window it lands in.\n- Result: rate-limiter killed up to **1.06 MB/s of starboard camera bytes\n  per second** at peak (§1.1 of `2026-05-01_findings.md`).\n- All four cameras share an identical GOP of 30 frames and start in phase\n  → IDRs coincide roughly **every 6 seconds**, all run.\n\n## Current encoder config\n\n`bizzyboat_project11/launch/oak_cameras_launch.py` L9–18: all four cameras\nat `h265_bitrate_kbps: 800`. `h265_keyframe_frequency_frames` is not set\n→ depthai_marine default **30 frames**. `fps` is not set → camera_base\ndefault **5.0 fps**. → **6 s GOP, identical across all four cameras**.\n\nRate-control mode is not set → inherits depthai property default **CBR**\n(averages over a window, not per-frame).\n\n## Proposal\n\nSet per-camera **coprime** `h265_keyframe_frequency_frames` so IDRs drift\nout of phase and rarely coincide. Lowest-importance cameras get the longest\nGOP (longer freeze on packet loss matters least there):\n\n| Camera | Priority | Current | **Proposed (frames)** | GOP @ 5 fps |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| oak_forward   | highest (primary nav view)  | 30 | **23** | 4.6 s |\n| oak_port      | situational awareness       | 30 | **29** | 5.8 s |\n| oak_starboard | situational awareness       | 30 | **31** | 6.2 s |\n| oak_aft       | **lowest** (least critical) | 30 | **37** | 7.4 s |\n\n23/29/31/37 are pairwise coprime primes near today's 30. Worst-case\nviewer-freeze on packet loss is bounded by per-camera GOP (4.6 s for the\ncritical forward view; 7.4 s only for the de-prioritized aft view). The\nLCM is ~30 hours, so coincidences are essentially eliminated.\n\nWhy coprime (not explicit stagger): the depthai v2.x API has **no GOP-phase\noffset** and **no runtime `forceIntraRequest`** on `dai::node::VideoEncoder`.\nCoprime intervals are the only API-supported way to decorrelate keyframes\nacross independent encoder instances.\n\nNo code changes needed — `h265_keyframe_frequency_frames` is already a ROS\nparam surface on `depthai_marine::CameraBase`. The change is a dict-literal\npatch to `oak_cameras_launch.py`.\n\n## Why not \"longer GOP\" as well\n\nWe are already at 6 s GOP at fps=5. Going further (12+ s) would push\nviewer-freeze beyond useful — operators wouldn't be able to tell a frozen\ncamera from a real outage. Aft's 7.4 s under this proposal is the longest\nacceptable; everything more critical stays under it.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [ ] Bench: launch all four cameras on a dev OAK, confirm the\n      per-camera keyframe frequencies stick (parse `is_keyframe` /\n      `flags` from `ffmpeg_image_transport` packets).\n- [ ] Bag a short underway segment with the new config, re-run the\n      `2026-05-01_network_perlink.py`-style per-second peak query, confirm\n      coincident-peak events drop to near-zero.\n- [ ] Verify viewer recovery time after an induced packet loss is within\n      the per-camera GOP budget.\n\n## Out of scope / referenced\n\n- udp_bridge resend-layer rework (in flight separately — addresses the\n  resend-storm dynamic seen in §1.5 of `2026-05-01_findings.md`).\n- Range sweep of bandwidth tests, sustained Starlink UDP loss\n  characterization — separate follow-ups under #124.\n\n## References\n\n- `~/data/analysis/2026-05-01_findings.md` §1.1, §1.5\n- `~/data/analysis/2026-05-18_starlink_only_budget.md`\n- Parent post-mission tracker: rolker/unh_echoboats_project11#124\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}